1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,416
Thursday, the twelfth of November.

2
00:00:02,436 --> 00:00:05,215
Nvidia's chief executive
chair is empty...

3
00:00:05,235 --> 00:00:08,560
and the person now in charge
is still at her old desk.

4
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:13,507
Colette Kress, the finance chief, has
just been named interim chief executive.

5
00:00:13,527 --> 00:00:16,660
Harvey Jones becomes the
board's first-ever chair.

6
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:17,710
Who's in the chair?

7
00:00:17,730 --> 00:00:19,660
Nobody. That's the news.

8
00:00:19,690 --> 00:00:23,842
By Thursday's close, the market put
this company at about four trillion

9
00:00:23,862 --> 00:00:26,190
dollars. Keep that number in mind.

10
00:00:26,210 --> 00:00:30,020
A slice of it sits in
every S&P 500 index fund.

11
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:35,268
And on her desk... a date. Tuesday:
the first results call since Nvidia

12
00:00:35,288 --> 00:00:38,830
went public... with Jensen
Huang gone from the top job.

13
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:40,350
Wait. Who's running Nvidia?

14
00:00:40,370 --> 00:00:41,490
The money person.

15
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:42,260
Is that good?

16
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:43,480
Ask me Tuesday.

17
00:00:43,510 --> 00:00:47,140
By the end, you'll know exactly
what to listen for at four twenty.

18
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:49,195
So who is running Nvidia now...

19
00:00:49,215 --> 00:00:50,940
and why does Tuesday matter?

20
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:54,079
It starts on Sunday,
the eighth of November.

21
00:00:54,099 --> 00:00:57,154
Nvidia's board opens an
independent review...

22
00:00:57,174 --> 00:01:01,589
in light of reporting that Jensen
Huang had been the target of a foreign

23
00:01:01,609 --> 00:01:02,870
intelligence effort.

24
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:07,509
The board's word is target. The claim
itself is an allegation, as reported,

25
00:01:07,529 --> 00:01:08,920
and China denies it.

26
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:11,987
Monday, the shares fall
about four percent.

27
00:01:12,007 --> 00:01:14,680
Tuesday, about five and a half more.

28
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:16,020
Nine and a half percent?

29
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:17,070
Over a review.

30
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:17,980
Not a finding?

31
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,430
I don't wait for
findings. I sold Monday.

32
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:21,720
So what's the question?

33
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:23,020
The one on the door.

34
00:01:23,050 --> 00:01:26,505
That was Sunday. By Thursday,
the question had moved...

35
00:01:26,525 --> 00:01:28,840
from what happened,
to who's in charge.

36
00:01:29,830 --> 00:01:32,100
To see why that hurts,
count his seats.

37
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:35,614
Jensen Huang founded Nvidia
thirty-three years ago.

38
00:01:35,634 --> 00:01:38,727
He was its chief
executive. Its president.

39
00:01:38,747 --> 00:01:40,400
And a member of its board.

40
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:43,010
And no successor had
been named in public.

41
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:46,963
For investors, that was
simple: one face, one voice.

42
00:01:46,983 --> 00:01:51,070
It also meant that if he ever left,
three seats would empty at once.

43
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:52,440
So who was next in line?

44
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:53,420
Nobody said.

45
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:54,580
Did anybody ask?

46
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:55,900
Everybody. Now.

47
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:57,060
Who chairs your board?

48
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:57,737
Nobody.

49
00:01:57,757 --> 00:01:58,417
Nobody?

50
00:01:58,437 --> 00:01:59,587
It never came up.

51
00:01:59,607 --> 00:02:00,467
It will.

52
00:02:00,497 --> 00:02:04,231
Until this week, Nvidia's
board had no chair at all.

53
00:02:04,251 --> 00:02:08,087
The man at the top held the company's
three biggest seats himself.

54
00:02:08,107 --> 00:02:13,474
Gil Luria of DA Davidson told Fox
Business that much of the decision-making

55
00:02:13,494 --> 00:02:15,297
was concentrated in Huang.

56
00:02:15,317 --> 00:02:17,845
So the risk was never
only the reports.

57
00:02:17,865 --> 00:02:20,507
It was the empty seats
they could leave behind.

58
00:02:20,537 --> 00:02:22,097
Three tickets. One coat.

59
00:02:22,117 --> 00:02:23,212
Is that allowed?

60
00:02:23,232 --> 00:02:23,962
Allowed.

61
00:02:23,982 --> 00:02:24,382
Wise?

62
00:02:24,402 --> 00:02:25,362
Different counter.

63
00:02:25,382 --> 00:02:27,072
One guy, three jobs? Efficient.

64
00:02:27,092 --> 00:02:28,762
Until he leaves all three.

65
00:02:28,782 --> 00:02:30,012
Who leaves all three?

66
00:02:30,032 --> 00:02:30,982
Keep lifting.

67
00:02:31,012 --> 00:02:35,686
On Friday, the sixth, the market
valued Nvidia at about four and a half

68
00:02:35,706 --> 00:02:37,042
trillion dollars.

69
00:02:37,072 --> 00:02:37,772
Big week?

70
00:02:37,792 --> 00:02:39,342
Huge. Nothing can go wrong.

71
00:02:39,362 --> 00:02:40,432
It's only Friday.

72
00:02:40,462 --> 00:02:41,972
And then came Wednesday.

73
00:02:42,962 --> 00:02:47,877
Wednesday, the eleventh. The shares
close up almost three percent, at about

74
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:50,142
one hundred and seventy-four dollars.

75
00:02:50,162 --> 00:02:53,552
For a few hours, it looked
as if the worst was over.

76
00:02:53,582 --> 00:02:54,612
Green! We're back!

77
00:02:54,632 --> 00:02:56,072
The bell rang, Chad.

78
00:02:56,092 --> 00:02:56,602
So?

79
00:02:56,622 --> 00:02:58,079
So the night shift starts.

80
00:02:58,109 --> 00:03:01,492
After the close, two
newspapers publish reports.

81
00:03:01,512 --> 00:03:03,709
The market reads both overnight.

82
00:03:03,729 --> 00:03:08,163
The New York Times reports a second
relationship, with a senior Nvidia

83
00:03:08,183 --> 00:03:10,299
executive who reports to Huang.

84
00:03:10,319 --> 00:03:13,257
Neither Nvidia nor
Huang had confirmed it.

85
00:03:13,277 --> 00:03:16,849
We're not saying it's true.
We're saying it was reported.

86
00:03:16,869 --> 00:03:21,069
The Wall Street Journal reports that
the board is preparing to remove him.

87
00:03:21,099 --> 00:03:21,989
So it's true?

88
00:03:22,009 --> 00:03:22,989
Allegation.

89
00:03:23,009 --> 00:03:24,019
So it's false?

90
00:03:24,039 --> 00:03:25,079
Allegation.

91
00:03:25,099 --> 00:03:26,009
So what is it?

92
00:03:26,029 --> 00:03:26,769
Reported.

93
00:03:26,789 --> 00:03:28,749
Down eight percent? After the bell?

94
00:03:28,769 --> 00:03:31,139
After-hours, bro. Market never sleeps.

95
00:03:31,159 --> 00:03:32,049
I'd like to.

96
00:03:32,079 --> 00:03:36,019
In after-hours trading, the shares
fall eight to nine percent.

97
00:03:36,039 --> 00:03:39,426
So on Wednesday night,
two clocks ran at once.

98
00:03:39,446 --> 00:03:41,709
The board's... and the market's.

99
00:03:41,739 --> 00:03:43,249
Two reports in one night.

100
00:03:43,269 --> 00:03:44,829
Neither confirmed that night.

101
00:03:44,849 --> 00:03:46,239
So it's not proven.

102
00:03:46,259 --> 00:03:48,039
Not proven. Still priced.

103
00:03:48,069 --> 00:03:52,819
And according to Nvidia's own regulatory
filing, the board accepted his

104
00:03:52,839 --> 00:03:55,149
resignation that same Wednesday.

105
00:03:55,169 --> 00:03:57,489
Nvidia would announce it on Thursday.

106
00:03:58,479 --> 00:04:01,206
Thursday. Nvidia puts out a release.

107
00:04:01,226 --> 00:04:04,534
The board announces that
Jensen Huang has resigned...

108
00:04:04,674 --> 00:04:10,139
as president, chief executive and a member
of the board, effective immediately.

109
00:04:10,159 --> 00:04:12,045
The release gives no reason.

110
00:04:12,065 --> 00:04:15,849
And no handover date: effective
immediately means now.

111
00:04:15,869 --> 00:04:19,003
Some founders step down
and stay on as chairman.

112
00:04:19,062 --> 00:04:21,379
Not here. He has no seat left.

113
00:04:21,409 --> 00:04:23,019
One plate, please. Says boss.

114
00:04:23,039 --> 00:04:24,439
Permanent or temporary?

115
00:04:24,459 --> 00:04:25,489
What's the difference?

116
00:04:25,509 --> 00:04:26,279
Tape.

117
00:04:26,309 --> 00:04:31,233
The chief executive's job goes to
Colette Kress, Nvidia's finance chief

118
00:04:31,253 --> 00:04:35,870
since 2013. Her new title:
interim chief executive...

119
00:04:35,890 --> 00:04:38,330
and chief financial officer.

120
00:04:38,350 --> 00:04:39,549
She keeps both.

121
00:04:39,569 --> 00:04:45,718
Harvey Jones, on the board since 1993
and a former chief executive of Synopsys,

122
00:04:45,738 --> 00:04:48,459
becomes Nvidia's
first-ever board chair.

123
00:04:48,489 --> 00:04:49,499
Who's in the chair?

124
00:04:49,519 --> 00:04:51,509
Which chair? There are two now.

125
00:04:51,529 --> 00:04:52,519
Is that better?

126
00:04:52,539 --> 00:04:53,629
It's more chairs.

127
00:04:53,659 --> 00:04:57,677
In the release, Jones says the board
has "full confidence in Colette and

128
00:04:57,697 --> 00:05:02,476
the leadership team". The release
adds, "Jensen's contributions to NVIDIA

129
00:05:02,496 --> 00:05:03,679
are profound."

130
00:05:03,699 --> 00:05:08,853
And Kress says, "Our focus remains
on serving our customers, partners,

131
00:05:08,873 --> 00:05:11,039
developers and shareholders."

132
00:05:11,069 --> 00:05:12,339
Does it say why?

133
00:05:12,359 --> 00:05:13,519
It says thank you.

134
00:05:13,539 --> 00:05:14,649
That's not why.

135
00:05:14,669 --> 00:05:15,999
Both sides checked.

136
00:05:16,029 --> 00:05:21,044
The board starts a search for a permanent
chief executive, looking at candidates

137
00:05:21,064 --> 00:05:23,359
inside and outside the company.

138
00:05:23,389 --> 00:05:25,939
Wanted: one chief executive.

139
00:05:25,959 --> 00:05:26,789
Experience?

140
00:05:26,809 --> 00:05:28,439
Running four trillion dollars.

141
00:05:28,459 --> 00:05:29,799
I ran a lemonade stand.

142
00:05:29,819 --> 00:05:30,549
Next.

143
00:05:30,579 --> 00:05:34,991
So for now, Nvidia is run by
the person who counts its money.

144
00:05:35,011 --> 00:05:35,949
Who is she?

145
00:05:36,939 --> 00:05:41,898
Colette Kress isn't new. She joined
Nvidia as finance chief in September

146
00:05:41,918 --> 00:05:43,059
2013.

147
00:05:43,079 --> 00:05:46,009
She's fifty-nine,
according to Yahoo Finance.

148
00:05:46,029 --> 00:05:51,520
Before that job, she ran finance for
a unit at Cisco, and for Microsoft's

149
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:55,814
server and tools division. Nearly
twenty-five years in finance at tech

150
00:05:55,834 --> 00:05:56,619
companies.

151
00:05:56,649 --> 00:05:58,952
A quarter century under the hood.

152
00:05:58,972 --> 00:05:59,712
And now?

153
00:05:59,732 --> 00:06:01,162
Now she drives it too.

154
00:06:01,182 --> 00:06:02,422
Then who's under the hood?

155
00:06:02,452 --> 00:06:06,170
On Nvidia's earnings calls, she's the
one who normally walks through the

156
00:06:06,190 --> 00:06:06,882
numbers.

157
00:06:06,912 --> 00:06:08,747
Two jobs at once? I can't do one.

158
00:06:08,767 --> 00:06:09,497
We know.

159
00:06:09,527 --> 00:06:11,720
Here's the part few people noticed.

160
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:13,743
The person who signs the numbers...

161
00:06:13,763 --> 00:06:16,800
reads them to the market...
and now runs the company.

162
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:17,787
Same person.

163
00:06:17,817 --> 00:06:19,067
So who's in the chair?

164
00:06:19,087 --> 00:06:20,288
The one with the calculator.

165
00:06:20,308 --> 00:06:21,228
Is that normal?

166
00:06:21,248 --> 00:06:23,528
Ask four analysts. Get five opinions.

167
00:06:23,558 --> 00:06:25,895
Fox Business asked four of them.

168
00:06:25,915 --> 00:06:31,205
One: Susannah Streeter of Wealth Club
calls her a "safe pair of hands"...

169
00:06:31,225 --> 00:06:36,448
but says she lacks Huang's global
status and his deep commercial ties.

170
00:06:36,468 --> 00:06:41,880
Two: Dan Ives of Yorkville
Ives says, "we are big fans".

171
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:47,398
Three: Bret Kenwell of eToro expects her
to "provide stability and reassurance".

172
00:06:47,418 --> 00:06:52,589
Four: Gil Luria says the task is a
heavy one, because so much ran through

173
00:06:52,609 --> 00:06:53,198
Huang.

174
00:06:53,228 --> 00:06:55,182
Safe pair of hands. Big fans.

175
00:06:55,202 --> 00:06:56,134
Stability.

176
00:06:56,154 --> 00:06:57,944
Notice the word nobody used.

177
00:06:57,964 --> 00:06:58,614
Which?

178
00:06:58,634 --> 00:06:59,444
Permanent.

179
00:06:59,474 --> 00:07:02,562
The analysts were calm.
The market was not.

180
00:07:03,552 --> 00:07:07,832
Thursday, the shares fell more
than twelve percent at one point.

181
00:07:07,862 --> 00:07:09,252
Down twelve! Buy the dip!

182
00:07:09,272 --> 00:07:10,542
It's climbing back.

183
00:07:10,562 --> 00:07:11,332
Sell the rip!

184
00:07:11,352 --> 00:07:12,512
Pick one, Chad.

185
00:07:12,542 --> 00:07:14,771
Remember that four trillion dollars?

186
00:07:14,791 --> 00:07:17,342
The Friday before, it
was four and a half.

187
00:07:17,362 --> 00:07:21,605
Between those two dates, about four
hundred and ninety billion dollars

188
00:07:21,625 --> 00:07:23,452
of market value disappeared.

189
00:07:23,472 --> 00:07:27,762
That's our subtraction, from figures
in Yahoo Finance's live blog.

190
00:07:27,782 --> 00:07:31,956
That's more than four times what
analysts expect Nvidia to sell in the

191
00:07:31,976 --> 00:07:35,402
whole quarter. Our
arithmetic, not a forecast.

192
00:07:35,422 --> 00:07:40,120
The neighbours shook too. On Thursday
morning, CoreWeave was down about

193
00:07:40,140 --> 00:07:44,712
nine percent and Nebius about
sixteen, according to TechCrunch.

194
00:07:44,742 --> 00:07:46,052
What happened to you two?

195
00:07:46,072 --> 00:07:47,432
We stood next to him.

196
00:07:47,452 --> 00:07:48,192
That's it?

197
00:07:48,212 --> 00:07:48,842
That's enough.

198
00:07:48,872 --> 00:07:53,273
Friday, the thirteenth.
HSBC cuts Nvidia to Hold...

199
00:07:53,293 --> 00:07:57,638
and its price target from three hundred
and sixty-five dollars to one hundred

200
00:07:57,658 --> 00:07:58,552
and seventy.

201
00:07:58,572 --> 00:08:01,332
The new target is less
than half the old one.

202
00:08:01,352 --> 00:08:06,439
Its analyst, Frank Lee, said confidence
was "shaken", as the "leadership

203
00:08:06,459 --> 00:08:08,406
succession plan is unclear".

204
00:08:08,426 --> 00:08:10,092
That's one bank's view.

205
00:08:10,112 --> 00:08:14,803
Michael Landsberg of Landsberg Bennett
called the resignation "the worst-case

206
00:08:14,823 --> 00:08:18,272
scenario" in the saga,
Fox Business reports.

207
00:08:18,302 --> 00:08:19,022
Hold.

208
00:08:19,042 --> 00:08:19,702
Hold what?

209
00:08:19,722 --> 00:08:20,602
Your breath.

210
00:08:20,632 --> 00:08:25,402
On Friday, the shares closed at about
one hundred and sixty-three dollars.

211
00:08:25,422 --> 00:08:29,272
StoneX puts them about thirty
percent below their record high.

212
00:08:29,302 --> 00:08:30,812
And who's in the chair after her?

213
00:08:30,832 --> 00:08:32,752
That's the bit nobody can tell you.

214
00:08:32,782 --> 00:08:34,652
Which is why Tuesday matters.

215
00:08:35,642 --> 00:08:37,359
So where does this touch you?

216
00:08:37,379 --> 00:08:39,232
In a fund you may never open.

217
00:08:39,252 --> 00:08:44,946
Before the fall, about seven dollars
of every hundred in an S&P 500 index

218
00:08:44,966 --> 00:08:49,174
fund was Nvidia. After this
week the slice is smaller.

219
00:08:49,194 --> 00:08:51,222
We haven't seen a current figure.

220
00:08:51,252 --> 00:08:52,592
Do you own Nvidia?

221
00:08:52,612 --> 00:08:53,202
No.

222
00:08:53,222 --> 00:08:54,782
Own an index fund?

223
00:08:54,802 --> 00:08:55,252
...Yes.

224
00:08:55,272 --> 00:08:56,752
Then you own Nvidia.

225
00:08:56,772 --> 00:08:58,092
Wait. I own that?

226
00:08:58,122 --> 00:09:03,670
Which means a company inside your
401(k) is run by a finance executive

227
00:09:03,690 --> 00:09:05,302
on a temporary title.

228
00:09:05,322 --> 00:09:09,585
Whether you're thirty or sixty, if
you own the index, you own a piece of

229
00:09:09,605 --> 00:09:10,542
this question.

230
00:09:10,562 --> 00:09:13,049
Tuesday, the seventeenth of November.

231
00:09:13,069 --> 00:09:17,710
At about four twenty p.m. Eastern,
Nvidia releases its results for the

232
00:09:17,730 --> 00:09:20,832
quarter that ended on the
twenty-fifth of October.

233
00:09:20,852 --> 00:09:26,033
At five, the call begins. Kress's
written finance commentary goes up right

234
00:09:26,053 --> 00:09:27,142
after the release.

235
00:09:27,162 --> 00:09:31,667
Notice the dates. Tuesday's numbers
cover a quarter that closed before

236
00:09:31,687 --> 00:09:33,342
the board's review began.

237
00:09:33,372 --> 00:09:34,922
So the numbers are from before?

238
00:09:34,942 --> 00:09:36,862
Before. The voice is after.

239
00:09:36,882 --> 00:09:37,982
What time's the game?

240
00:09:38,002 --> 00:09:39,462
Four twenty. Eastern.

241
00:09:39,482 --> 00:09:40,252
Who's playing?

242
00:09:40,272 --> 00:09:41,932
One person. Two jobs.

243
00:09:41,962 --> 00:09:45,511
Only analysts and big
investors get to ask questions.

244
00:09:45,531 --> 00:09:49,092
Anyone can listen on the webcast
at Nvidia's investor site.

245
00:09:49,122 --> 00:09:50,332
Can I ask a question?

246
00:09:50,352 --> 00:09:50,812
No.

247
00:09:50,832 --> 00:09:51,632
Can I listen?

248
00:09:51,652 --> 00:09:52,662
Yes. Quietly.

249
00:09:52,692 --> 00:09:56,857
It's the first Nvidia call without
Jensen Huang as chief executive...

250
00:09:56,877 --> 00:09:59,442
and Kress's first as interim boss.

251
00:09:59,462 --> 00:10:04,333
StoneX's Matt Weller calls this report
Nvidia's first test since Huang

252
00:10:04,353 --> 00:10:04,842
left.

253
00:10:04,872 --> 00:10:06,582
So what do I do on Tuesday?

254
00:10:06,602 --> 00:10:07,222
Listen.

255
00:10:07,242 --> 00:10:07,882
That's it?

256
00:10:07,902 --> 00:10:09,863
That's more than most people do.

257
00:10:09,893 --> 00:10:12,094
This isn't advice to buy or sell.

258
00:10:12,114 --> 00:10:13,833
It's a date on the calendar.

259
00:10:13,853 --> 00:10:16,585
So when the numbers land,
here's what to look for.

260
00:10:16,605 --> 00:10:17,623
Three things.

261
00:10:18,613 --> 00:10:23,715
One: the big number. Analysts expect
revenue of about one hundred and nine

262
00:10:23,735 --> 00:10:27,122
billion dollars for the
quarter, according to StoneX.

263
00:10:27,142 --> 00:10:29,003
That's three months of sales.

264
00:10:29,023 --> 00:10:34,007
Nvidia's own forecast, given in August,
was one hundred and eight billion,

265
00:10:34,027 --> 00:10:38,163
give or take two percent. Both
are estimates, not results.

266
00:10:38,183 --> 00:10:43,272
Two details in that August fine print:
Nvidia guided its gross margin to

267
00:10:43,292 --> 00:10:48,027
about seventy-four percent. And it
assumed no data-center chip sales to

268
00:10:48,047 --> 00:10:48,753
China.

269
00:10:48,783 --> 00:10:50,173
Seventy-four percent of what?

270
00:10:50,193 --> 00:10:53,313
Of each sales dollar, left
after making the product.

271
00:10:53,333 --> 00:10:54,663
That's a lot of dollar.

272
00:10:54,683 --> 00:10:55,732
What if they beat it?

273
00:10:55,752 --> 00:10:57,142
Then we listen to part two.

274
00:10:57,162 --> 00:10:58,192
And if they miss?

275
00:10:58,212 --> 00:10:59,432
Then we listen harder.

276
00:10:59,462 --> 00:11:02,138
Two: the forecast
for the next quarter.

277
00:11:02,158 --> 00:11:05,272
It's the first one given
with Kress as interim boss.

278
00:11:05,302 --> 00:11:06,642
I see... fog.

279
00:11:06,662 --> 00:11:07,747
That's the guidance.

280
00:11:07,767 --> 00:11:08,947
Is it good fog?

281
00:11:08,967 --> 00:11:10,092
Ask me Tuesday.

282
00:11:10,122 --> 00:11:12,692
Three: any word on the search.

283
00:11:12,712 --> 00:11:17,517
As of Saturday, the fourteenth of
November, no permanent chief executive

284
00:11:17,537 --> 00:11:18,492
has been named.

285
00:11:18,512 --> 00:11:22,973
On Friday, the Financial Times reported,
as relayed by Reuters, that the

286
00:11:22,993 --> 00:11:26,534
board had approached an executive
at another chip company.

287
00:11:26,554 --> 00:11:31,331
Nvidia's board says it has not formally
engaged any candidate, and calls

288
00:11:31,351 --> 00:11:34,025
speculation about names "premature".

289
00:11:34,045 --> 00:11:35,372
It's one report.

290
00:11:35,402 --> 00:11:36,672
Is that the new boss?

291
00:11:36,692 --> 00:11:38,512
It's a shadow with a phone.

292
00:11:38,542 --> 00:11:43,289
In the same release, Jones says the
board expects "the company's operations,

293
00:11:43,309 --> 00:11:48,152
customer commitments and product roadmap
to continue without interruption".

294
00:11:48,172 --> 00:11:52,588
As of Saturday, we found no statement
from Huang on his resignation.

295
00:11:52,608 --> 00:11:56,342
And no report we read says whether
the board's review goes on.

296
00:11:56,372 --> 00:11:57,935
Who's in the chair on Tuesday?

297
00:11:57,955 --> 00:11:58,695
She is.

298
00:11:58,715 --> 00:11:59,704
And the stock?

299
00:11:59,724 --> 00:12:01,764
Nobody knows that yet. Including us.

300
00:12:01,794 --> 00:12:05,856
And here's the twist. On Tuesday,
the person reporting the quarter...

301
00:12:05,876 --> 00:12:08,964
is also the person the
market is judging by it.

302
00:12:08,994 --> 00:12:10,774
Interim. And a question mark.

303
00:12:10,794 --> 00:12:11,674
And a clock.

304
00:12:11,694 --> 00:12:12,754
So who's in the chair?

305
00:12:12,774 --> 00:12:14,764
For now? The one with the calculator.

306
00:12:14,794 --> 00:12:16,929
So who is running Nvidia now?

307
00:12:16,988 --> 00:12:20,014
Its finance chief, on
a temporary title.

308
00:12:20,034 --> 00:12:21,686
And why does Tuesday matter?

309
00:12:21,706 --> 00:12:25,646
Because at four twenty, the numbers
and the new boss arrive together...

310
00:12:25,666 --> 00:12:26,714
for the first time.
